diff --git a/doc/FPVIEW.md b/doc/FPVIEW.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..64e4031
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/FPVIEW.md
@@ -0,0 +1,142 @@
+# 3D domain views (FP)
+
+A native-only mod for the PC port: extra cameras for the domains, with the camera moving
+smoothly between them. **F4** cycles the views, also in **F1 > Forbs Hidden Mods > Domain camera**:
+
+1. **Classic**: the original overhead view, drawn by the game's own renderer.
+2. **High chase**: raised behind the Digi-Beetle, looking down, turning with it.
+3. **Low chase**: just behind and above the beetle, third person.
+4. **First person**: the camera in the beetle.
+
+Each press swoops the camera to the next view in under a second (height, distance, tilt,
+field of view and rotation blend together; the overhead view's squashed tile rows unsquash
+on the way). The choice is saved as `domain_view` in `settings.ini` (0..3); `--fpview`
+starts in first person. **F10** turns ceilings on and off. **Smooth turning** (F1 > Forbs Hidden Mods,
+on by default, `smooth_turns`) eases the camera and the beetle round when it turns.
+**Mystery encounters** (F1 > Forbs Hidden Mods, `mystery_encounters`) show wandering Digimon as a
+floating ? until the battle starts: 1 per floor, 30% of encounters, all except bosses, or off
+(the default). It works in every view, Classic too. **A ? holds a rarer Digimon** (F1, on by
+default, `mystery_rare`) swaps each ? for one of its floor's less common enemy groups.
+**LvlLimitBoostr**: a rare one-use find that raises a party Digimon's level limit by 1 (see
+below), in 1% of all chests and 1% of won ? encounters (F1 checkboxes, on by default).
+
+## Controls in the 3D views
+
+Classic keeps the original controls. The other views use these; the controls switch when
+a transition ends, never half way.
+
+| Pad | Does |
+| --- | --- |
+| Up | Step forward |
+| Left / Right | Turn 90 degrees on the spot |
+| Down | Turn around |
+| Up + Left / Right | Step diagonally forward (the view keeps facing ahead) |
+| Cross, Square, Select ... | As before; Cross acts on the tile you face |
+
+The beetle turns back to the view after a diagonal step, so Cross always acts on what is in
+front of you.
+
+## How it works
+
+Everything is presentation. Turns, RNG, the retail draw window and the game's own state run as
+before, and the PS1 matching build is untouched: every change is inside `#ifdef DW2_NATIVE`
+(the retail-preprocessed sources of the five touched stag4000 files are identical).
+
+- **Camera** (`Stg40_FpCameraTick`, called by `Stg40_CameraUpdate` after the retail
+  `GsSetRefView2`): the retail camera always runs (its scripts too; the game waits on
+  `Stg40_CamIsMoving`) and is read back from `GsWSMATRIX` as the classic pose. A pose is a
+  pivot over the beetle, a distance back from it, yaw, pitch, projection, tile-row depth and
+  fog (`Stg40FpPose`); a transition blends the pose it started from into the live target
+  pose, eased. Classic settled: the retail renderer and camera, untouched.
+- **Smooth turning**: the camera yaw and the beetle's drawn rotation (`Stg40_FpDrawHeading`)
+  ease toward the game's heading, 20% of the way left each frame (`FP_TURN_EASE`). The game's
+  own heading, turn timing and move logic are untouched. Off: the original snap (the camera
+  at the game's 0x100 a frame).
+- **Battle exits and floor changes**: `Stg40_FpReset` (each floor set-up) drops any transition;
+  the camera snaps to the chosen view at its next use. The overlay reload after a battle
+  resets this state too.
+- **Floor** (`Stg40_FpDrawFloor`, replacing the retail 9x9 window): every cell out to
+  `STG40_FP_RADIUS` tiles, nearest first. Each tile top and wall is clipped in world space
+  against the near plane and a guard band around the screen, then projected by the GTE vertex
+  by vertex (so the PGXP hook records them and the HD renderer draws them perspective-correct),
+  and sent as `POLY_GT3` fans, faded to black with distance. The packets go into the mod's own
+  double-buffered area, a static array inside the 16 MB window the 24-bit OT links reach, so
+  the floor never takes room from the game's models. Without precise geometry (no wobble and
+  smooth frames both off) floors are cut 2 x 2 and walls 2 x 1 against affine texture warp.
+- **Ceilings** on walled floors (walls going up): each floor tile gets a ceiling at the wall
+  tops with its own floor texture, a little darker (`FP_CEILING_SHADE`). Off by default;
+  **F10** or F1 > Forbs Hidden Mods > Ceilings in first person turns them on (`fp_ceilings` in
+  `settings.ini`).
+  Platform floors have no walls above them, so they stay open to the black background.
+- **Walls** keep the retail templates and the retail cull rule (`Stg40_DrawTileWalls`), so
+  walled floors show walls from inside, and platform floors (cliffs going down) show the
+  cliffs that face you.
+- **Square tiles**: retail rows are 0x800 deep and columns 0xA00 wide (made for the angled
+  overhead camera). In first person both are 0xA00, and entity positions go through
+  `Stg40_FpWorldX / Z` to match. The FP world is centred on the beetle.
+- **Minimap pointer**: in the views that turn with the beetle, its own marker square on both automap views
+  gets a small white triangle on the side the view faces, turning with it
+  (`Stg40_FpDrawMapArrow`).
+- **Entities** beyond the retail 5-tile window are drawn through the 16:9 side models
+  (out to the radius). Models behind the eye are dropped by the GTE error flags per bone.
+- **The beetle** and the models linked to it are not drawn while the eye is within a tile of
+  it (first person, and a camera moving in); their anims still run.
+- **Solid cells** get black tops at wall height when the eye is above the walls, as the retail
+  floor draws them, so rock hides what lies behind it from the chase views.
+
+## Mystery encounters
+
+- **Rolls** (`Stg40_FpNoteParty`, `Stg40_FpRollMystery`, from `Stg40_SpawnEnemyParties` when a
+  floor is built): each enemy party gets its boss flag (`Enemy_GetSetSummary`) and a roll
+  0..99, and the floor gets one chosen non-boss party. The numbers come from the mod's own
+  generator (`Host_FpRandom`), never the game's, so battles, drops and encounters are the
+  game's. They are kept in host memory (`Host_FpGetNote`), since the dungeon overlay's data is
+  reset after every battle: a ? stays a ? until you fight it.
+- **Which show as ?** (`Stg40_FpMystery`, the setting read live): 1 the chosen party, 2 a roll
+  under 30, 3 every party; bosses never.
+- **Drawing**: the model is not drawn but its anim and texture anim run (the safe path the
+  beetle uses); its shadow stays and a yellow ? floats over it, bobbing, facing the camera
+  (`Stg40_FpDrawMystery`), in both the retail draw window and the far side models.
+- **Enemy info** (Square) leaves mystery parties off its list.
+- **Rarer Digimon** (`Stg40_FpSwapRarer`, when the floor is built, under the settings of that
+  moment): a floor has up to four enemy sets (`enemySets`), and every spawn point of every
+  layout of the floor lists four picks among them, so a set listed fewer times turns up less
+  often. A party that will show as a ? becomes a set the floor uses that is strictly rarer
+  than its own, weighted 1 / count toward the rarest; already the rarest, it stays. Never a
+  boss set. The party (Digimon, levels, AI, pace, gift) and its map model are filled in as
+  `Stg40_SpawnEnemyParties` fills a new one, so the floor's own table and levels hold.
+
+## LvlLimitBoostr
+
+- **What it does**: +1 to a party Digimon's level limit (`maxLevel`). Only for a Digimon at
+  its limit whose limit is below 99; a limit of 99 can't be raised.
+- **Where it comes from** (`FP_BOOST_PCT`, 1 %; F1 > Forbs Hidden Mods > LvlLimitBoostr, both on by
+  default):
+  - **1% of all chests** (`boost_chests`): every chest in the domains is rolled the first
+    time it is opened (`Stg40_FpChestHasBoost`), mystery encounters or not. None leaves it in
+    the chest, which stays closed (as with a full bag), to come back to; a chest is never
+    rolled twice (per-floor masks in host notes 11 and 14).
+  - **1% of all ? encounters** (`boost_battles`): a won ? battle offers it right after the
+    battle (`Stg40_FpNoteBattle` at the encounter, the battle stage's win path notes the win,
+    `Stg40_FpBattleBoostDue` back in the dungeon). None loses it.
+- **The prompt** (`Stg40_FpBoostPrompt`): a message box with the title, the selected party
+  Digimon (name, level / limit) or None, and a hint; Up / Down (or Left / Right) choose,
+  (X) confirms, (O) is None. A Digimon that can't take it says why. The text is the game's own
+  glyph encoding (`Stg40_FpGlyphs`, the table of `host/devedit.c`).
+- **Never in the bag**: used at once or left, so a save stays readable by the official port.
+
+## Tuning
+
+`src/stag4000/fpview_math.inc`: `FP_HIGH_*` and `FP_LOW_*` (chase cameras: pivot height,
+distance, pitch, projection), `FP_BLEND_FRAMES` (transition length), `FP_EYE` (eye height),
+`FP_H` (projection, about 80 degrees),
+`FP_PITCH` (look down), `FP_FOG_START / END`, `STG40_FP_RADIUS` (in `fpview.h`),
+`FP_TURN_STEP` (turn speed), `FP_MAP_ARROW` (minimap pointer size), `FP_MYSTERY_PCT`,
+`FP_MYSTERY_PIX` and `FP_MYSTERY_HEIGHT` (the 30 % setting, the ?'s size and height).
+
+## Known limits
+
+- Camera scripts (floor intro, scrolls to a target when shooting a Gift or reading enemy info)
+  are not shown; the view stays in the beetle.
+- Entities far from the beetle need their files in the CD cache, as the 16:9 side columns do.
+- Smooth frames do not blend the dungeon while in first person (30 fps there).
